    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm working on adding a small loft to a residential build out in Oregon. i'm looking at using a heavy Douglas Fir beam for the main support. if the span is 14 feet and i'm expecting a live load of about 40 psf plus the dead load of the loft itself, what kind of deflection limits should i be looking at to make sure the floor doesn't feel bouncy? also, what are some things i should look for in the timber itself to make sure it's actually going to hold the weight? thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For a 14-foot span residential loft in Oregon, standard engineering practice usually limits total deflection to L/360 (roughly 0.74 inches) under full live load to prevent a bouncy feel, though tighter limits like L/480 may be preferred by users sensitive to vibration. When selecting the Douglas Fir timber, prioritize clear, sturdy grade是一张 (like Select Structural) with verified knots that don't exceed 3 inches in size or location, as these defects significantly reduce load capacity. Always ensure the pair of beams are skew-cut at both ends to deflect nearly evenly, which helps mitigate uneven vibration that creates a nervous feeling underfoot. Finally, consult a local structural engineer to confirm these values, given Oregon's specific snow and wind loads and the critical nature of DIY build-outs.",
